t left live to signing.\n  Recommended: Keep exit-and-return: 'reengaging' implies they were out; close E on 08/04 when the Committee continued with only G&W and Party B (its $21.26 was below both). Alternative: treat both as continuously live (live count 4 from 08/01) or close E only at signing.\n  Why: 'no longer involved in the process' (p. 30); 'reengaging and enhancing their offers' (p. 30); 'continue diligence and negotiations with both G&W and Party B' (p. 31).\n  Rows affected: #37-#40, #43 (D/E exits and returns).\n  What changes: Live-bidder counts 07/27-08/12 (2 vs 4) and E's final exit label/date (Dropped 08/04 vs Not selected 08/12).\n\n[Q5]\n  Question: Party A - the first counterparty (Q4 2015) - is never mentioned after the memorandum stage; it may sit in the 16 IOI non-submitters, the two excluded low bidders, or the two non-submitting LOI bidders. No separate exit row is written.\n  Recommended: Keep no separate exit row; residual Notes say they may include Party A; treat Party A as exited by signing inside a residual (reason Not stated). All six LOI submitters are named, so Party A did not submit one.\n  Why: Party A named through the memo distribution (p. 28); the nine IOIs are unnamed (p. 29); all six LOI submitters are named (pp. 29-30).\n  Rows affected: #16, #17, #30 (residuals that may include Party A).\n  What changes: Only the composition of anonymous residuals; no prices, rounds or named exits change.\n\n====================================================================================================\nTarget: Providence and Worcester Railroad Company (PWRR), a Rhode Island corporation; regional freight railroad in Massachusetts, Rhode Island, Connecticut and New York; NASDAQ ticker PWX.\nAcquirer: Genesee & Wyoming Inc. (G&W), a Delaware corporation, through Pullman Acquisition Sub Inc., a wholly-owned subsidiary formed 08/12/2016.\nAcquirer type: Strategic - an operating freight-railroad company (owns or leases 121 freight railroads worldwide).\nAgreed price and consideration: $25.00 in cash per share of common stock, without interest; each preferred share converts automatically into 100 common shares (plus accrued/unpaid dividends) and then receives the same $25.00 cash; no financing condition.\nMerger agreement signed: 2016-08-12 00:00:00\nMerger announced: 2016-08-15 00:00:00\nFiling type and date: DEFM14A (definitive proxy statement), dated 09/19/2016; first mailed to shareholders on or about 09/21/2016.\nBackground pages: 27-31 (printed pages).\nInitiation: Bidder-led - Party A raised acquisition interest in Q4 2015; the Board then organized the process.\nNumber of processes: 1\nAuction screen: Met - 25 confidentiality agreements in the March-April wave (11 strategic, 14 financial); Party C signed a later agreement (see Q3).\nWhole-company bids: Yes - nine IOIs ($17.93-$26.50), six LOIs ($19.20-$24.00) and G&W's winning $25.00 cash bid; all were whole-company per-share bids.\nCurrency and units of bid prices: US dollars per share of common stock (the filing assumes conversion of preferred stock into common stock).\nTarget financial advisers: Greene Holcomb & Fisher LLC (GHF), engaged 01/27/2016; its business was acquired by BMO Capital Markets Corp. on 08/01/2016, which advised thereafter and delivered the fairness opinion (same adviser, no new row).\nTarget legal advisers: Hinckley, Allen & Snyder LLP (first seen acting 03/24/2016).\nAccount: PWRR, a New England regional freight railroad, explored a sale after Class I partner Party A raised acquisition interest in Q4 2015. The Board engaged GHF in January 2016 and in March approved a process combining discussions with Party A and a broad solicitation; 29 parties were contacted, 25 signed confidentiality agreements and nine submitted non-binding IOIs ($17.93-$26.50). Two low bidders were excluded and seven advanced to management presentations, producing six LOIs ($19.20-$24.00); G&W and Party B were selected for confirmatory diligence and definitive negotiation. G&W was told the Company intended to sign with Party B, then raised its all-cash offer to $25.00 per share on August 12; Party B declined to improve and the Board signed with G&W that day. The transaction was announced on August 15, 2016.\ntotal 32\ndrwxr-xr-x 2 82775 4012  4096 Sep 19 14:45 .\ndrwxr-xr-x 4 82775 4012  4096 Sep 19 14:34 ..\n-rw-r--r-- 1 82775 4012 21349 Sep 19 14:47 providence-worcester.xlsx\n"},{"type":"text","text":"Command exited with code 0."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"command\": \"cd /tmp/opencode && python3 - <<'EOF'\\nfrom openpyxl import load_workbook\\nwb = load_workbook('/home/uctpiaj/work/extraction/providence-worcester.xlsx')\\nprint(\\\"=\\\"*100)\\nfor r in wb['Questions'].iter_rows(min_row=2, values_only=True):\\n    print(\\\"\\\\n[\\\"+r[0]+\\\"]\\\")\\n    for label, val in zip([\\\"Question\\\",\\\"Recommended\\\",\\\"Why\\\",\\\"Rows affected\\\",\\\"What changes\\\"],[r[1],r[2],r[3],r[4],r[5]]):\\n        print(f\\\"  {label}: {val}\\\")\\nprint(\\\"\\\\n\\\"+\\\"=\\\"*100)\\nfor r in wb['Deal facts'].iter_rows(min_row=2, values_only=True):\\n    print(f\\\"{r[0]}: {r[1]}\\\")\\nEOF\\nls -la /home/uctpiaj/work/extraction/\"}"},"time":{"start":1789825627365,"end":1789825628159}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1789825628161,"sessionID":"ses_f461fdda8ffepr1o7xqEzpCNdg","part":{"id":"prt_0b9ebb801001pH8TLgftzhB1E5","sessionID":"ses_f461fdda8ffepr1o7xqEzpCNdg","messageID":"msg_0b9eba492001pntMdbdkydx448","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","cost":0.001155192,"tokens":{"input":380,"output":259,"reasoning":427,"cache":{"read":228864,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1789825630057,"sessionID":"ses_f461fdda8ffepr1o7xqEzpCNdg","part":{"id":"prt_0b9ebbf69001V9GanJWot1j9Rs","sessionID":"ses_f461fdda8ffepr1o7xqEzpCNdg","messageID":"msg_0b9ebb80c001KU6lQpVGiNYwVX","type":"step-start"}}
{"type":"text","timestamp":1789825637125,"sessionID":"ses_f461fdda8ffepr1o7xqEzpCNdg","part":{"id":"prt_0b9ebb80c001KU6lQpVGiNYwVX_text-0","sessionID":"ses_f461fdda8ffepr1o7xqEzpCNdg","messageID":"msg_0b9ebb80c001KU6lQpVGiNYwVX","type":"text","text":"The workbook is complete and verified. Here is the delivery per Part E of the instruction.\n\n## Workbook\n\n**`extraction/providence-worcester.xlsx`** — four sheets in order: **Deal ledger** (49 events), **Rounds** (3 rounds), **Questions** (5), **Deal facts** (16 fields). Header row frozen, filters on, wrapped text, no merged cells; date cells are real dates (MM/DD/YYYY); every quotation was machine-checked against the filing text and page-mapped to the printed page.\n\n## The sale, in brief\n\nProvidence & Worcester (a Rhode Island regional freight railroad) began exploring a sale in Q4 2015 after Class I partner \"Party A\" raised acquisition interest. The Board appointed a subcommittee, engaged GHF (Jan 27, 2016), and on March 14, 2016 approved a process mixing Party A talks with a broad solicitation. In the week of March 28, GHF contacted 11 strategic and 18 financial buyers; 25 signed confidentiality agreements (the auction screen is Met). Nine non-binding IOIs came in at $17.93–$26.50 (May 19–June 1); two low bidders were excluded and seven advanced to management presentations, producing six LOIs at $19.20–$24.00 in late July. G&W and Party B were chosen for confirmatory diligence and definitive negotiation on July 27. Party D and E briefly re-engaged with higher offers (Aug 1–2) but fell away; D withdrew, E reverted to $21.26 and was closed Aug 4. Told on Aug 9 that the Company intended to sign with Party B, G&W delivered a $25.00 all-cash revised LOI on Aug 12; Party B declined to raise its $24.00, and the Board signed with G&W that day (announced Aug 15, 2016).\n\n## Open Questions (with recommendations)\n\n1. **Round map** — one process, three rounds; R2 opens 06/01 (advancement + presentation/data-site stage), R3 opens 07/27 (definitive negotiation). *Recommended: keep; most plausible alternative is opening R2 at the mid-June LOI instruction, which moves only row #18.*\n2. **Deadline outcomes** — R1's 05/10 date was revised to 05/19 before arrival; 05/19 = *Late bids accepted* (IOIs received through 06/01); R2's 07/20 = *Late bids accepted* (G&W's 07/21 LOI considered). *Recommended: keep; alternatives are Enforced readings.*\n3. **Signer counts** — rows give 25 (11 strategic + 14 financial) in the March–April wave plus Party C (26), while the Board's reasons say 25; two extra Class I railroads were approached and never named again. *Recommended: wave = 25 as filed, Party C a separate 26th; the Class Is treated as non-entrants.*\n4. **Party D/E exits** — both treated as out on 07/27 (\"reengaging\" on 07/29), returning 08/01, with E closed 08/04. *Recommended: keep exit-and-return; live count 2 from 08/01; alternatives noted in Q4.*\n5. **Party A** — never mentioned after the memorandum; no separate exit row; residuals note it may include Party A. *Recommended: keep as a residual exit (by signing, reason Not stated).*\n\n## What I could not do\n\nThe filing never resolves: Party A's fate after the memo stage; the exact signing days of the 25 NDAs; the exact submission days of the six LOIs (only G&W's 07/21 is dated) and of the 05/10→05/19 postponement; whether D and E were formally told they were out; and the identities behind the anonymous cohorts (two low bidders, two LOI non-submitters, 16 non-submitting signers) — those remain cohort rows by design, per the instruction's conventions.","time":{"start":1789825628177,"end":1789825637125}}}
